            <ArticleTitle>Multiplexed fluctuation-dissipation-theorem calibration of optical tweezers inside living cells.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>113112</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1063/1.5012782</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In order to apply optical tweezers-based force measurements within an uncharacterized viscoelastic medium such as the cytoplasm of a living cell, a quantitative calibration method that may be applied in this complex environment is needed. We describe an improved version of the fluctuation-dissipation-theorem calibration method, which has been developed to perform in situ calibration in viscoelastic media without prior knowledge of the trapped object. Using this calibration procedure, it is possible to extract values of the medium's viscoelastic moduli as well as the force constant describing the optical trap. To demonstrate our method, we calibrate an optical trap in water, in polyethylene oxide solutions of different concentrations, and inside living fission yeast (S. pombe).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
